---
Correctness lens. I verified every disputed fact myself against the tree at
fix/phase-visibility. Only write to the tree is this verdict file.

1. THE CHECKER'S FACTUAL FINDING IS SUBSTANTIALLY CORRECT.
   docs/whatif-mcp-followups-2026-08-09.md:45-56 is "## 3. Data-directory
   resolution" and contains exactly two bullets: (a) cmd/whatif-mcp passes the
   settings dir to storage.New so the `.encrypted` marker is looked for in the
   wrong directory, and the MCP "reads age ciphertext as JSON and reports a
   parse error instead of 'storage is locked'"; (b) BUDGET_DATA_DIR is not
   honored. Neither bullet mentions the snapshot package, os.ReadFile, or a
   read failure. §3's encryption bug is a downstream JSON PARSE error, which
   presupposes the bytes were read successfully.
   snapshot.go:47-50 confirms the contradiction the checker names: "It copies
   the file's raw bytes with os.ReadFile rather than hard-linking it, so the
   backup is a point-in-time copy that a later in-place rewrite of the source
   cannot alter. When the store is encrypted the bytes are ciphertext, which is
   what a hand-restore back into that same store needs." Ciphertext is
   READABLE and copying it is the DESIRED behavior; the documented reason for
   ReadFile-over-Link is point-in-time immutability, not unreadability. So the
   comment at snapshot_test.go:126-131 ("blind copy of unreadable ciphertext
   must still fail loudly") is factually wrong and misdescribes both the cited
   doc and the code comment 11 lines above it. That much of the FAIL is right,
   and the finding is worth keeping.

   One correction to the checker's characterization, which matters because it
   is the stated GROUND of the FAIL: this is not "fabricated provenance." The
   document exists, §3 exists, and §3's first bullet genuinely is about this
   MCP not knowing a store is encrypted. What is wrong is an inference drawn
   from it, not an invented source. And the worker did not draw that inference.

2. THE ERROR ORIGINATES IN THE BRIEF, NOT THE WORK.
   `gh api repos/:owner/:repo/issues/26` body: "The reason Snapshotter.Ensure
   reads the source bytes rather than using os.Link is specifically the
   **unreadable** case: docs/whatif-mcp-followups-2026-08-09.md §3 records that
   this MCP detects encryption in the wrong directory, so a blind copy of
   ciphertext would appear to succeed and the caller's abort-before-write
   guarantee would silently not fire."
   .swarm/X-RUN-SPEC.md:52-58 relays it near-verbatim, citation and all. The
   worker's comment is that same claim with the same citation. The worker
   implemented the rationale the task gave it. The false claim entered the run
   upstream of dispatch and is currently sitting in issue #26 and in the SPEC.

3. AGAINST THIS TASK'S ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA, THE WORK PASSES.
   X1 attempt 2 (X-RUN-SPEC.md:52-58 + amendment :168-175 + D-2026-08-29e
   :42-45) requires: honest rename of the missing-file case; a genuinely
   unreadable case (source exists, read fails, error surfaced, no destination
   leaked); root-proof fixture (EISDIR-via-directory explicitly blessed);
   package-scope vet and tests green. Every one is met:
   - rename is honest (fixture writes no file; name now says Missing);
   - the unreadable fixture is os.Mkdir at the source path, so the path exists
     and os.ReadFile fails EISDIR at any uid — the exact pattern D-2026-08-29e
     named as acceptable, and the house pattern at
     internal/services/mcpsvc/curate/upsert_test.go:144;
   - I re-ran `go test -count=1 -v -run TestSnapshotter_FailsWhenSource
     ./internal/services/mcpsvc/snapshot/` myself: both PASS, ok 0.003s;
   - both lanes independently mutation-tested all three assertions (err,
     error-names-source, empty snapshot dir) and each bites; checker-second
     concedes in its own words that the construction is SOUND and that the
     "short-circuits earlier" attack does not hold;
   - scope is the single manifest file; snapshot.go is untouched.
   No acceptance criterion concerns the doc comment's citation. The FAIL rests
   entirely on a defect the criteria do not reach and the brief itself
   introduced.

4. WHY UPHOLD WOULD BE THE WRONG REMEDY.
   Sending this back charges the worker a failed attempt (hard-stop budget: 3)
   for the lead's own transcription of a wrong issue body. Worse, it is not
   actionable as a worker fix: the worker cannot resolve "the brief says X,
   the adjacent code comment says not-X" without the lead first deciding which
   is true. That decision is adjudication, which the constitution assigns to
   the lead, not to a re-dispatch. The correct remedy is a six-line doc-comment
   edit under "final review fixes," plus corrections that a worker could not
   make at all: issue #26's body and X-RUN-SPEC.md:52-58 both still assert the
   false rationale and will otherwise re-infect later work.

RULING: OVERRULE. The FAIL is set aside; the test as constructed satisfies
every stated acceptance criterion and is non-vacuous under two independent
mutation campaigns.

NOT A LICENSE TO LAND THE COMMENT. The finding is upheld even though the
verdict is not. Before commit the lead must (a) strip the
docs/whatif-mcp-followups-2026-08-09.md §3 citation and the encryption/
"unreadable ciphertext" tie-in from snapshot_test.go:126-131 and ground the
comment in Ensure's actual guarantee (snapshot.go:52-54, missing-or-unreadable
source must abort the caller's write) — note the house precedent at
upsert_test.go:138-143 cites no external doc at all, and this is the only .go
file in the repo referencing that document; (b) correct or annotate issue #26,
whose premise about why Ensure uses ReadFile is wrong; (c) record the
discrepancy in SPEC.md Rulings so the brief is not re-issued. If the lead
prefers the worker to make edit (a), it should be dispatched as a follow-on
doc-only task, not as a failed attempt against X1.
